$DIR/main.p:32:2: cannot pass f to a task statement: the new task may outlive n
    f is a function literal that uses n. A function literal that uses variables
    of the function around it refers to those variables where they live, in
    twice's frame (n is declared at main.p:29). The task started here runs on
    its own, and may still be running after twice returns and n's memory is
    reclaimed.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Pass a value, which is copied, or an owner, which moves, instead of a view
    of the frame.
    Or start it with a sync.Scope, which waits for its tasks before its block
    ends, so that they may use twice's variables:
        var s sync.Scope
        s.Task(func() { f() })
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3004" for a longer explanation)

